const guides = [
 {slug:"research-problem-statement",title:"How to Write a Research Problem Statement",desc:"A practical guide to defining a research problem, showing its significance and identifying the knowledge gap.",sections:[
 ["What is a research problem?","A research problem is a clearly described issue, uncertainty or gap in knowledge that a study is designed to investigate. It is narrower than a broad topic. For example, maternal health is a topic; uncertainty about factors associated with delayed antenatal booking in a defined population and setting is a researchable problem."],
 ["Build the problem logically","Start with the wider context and use credible, current evidence to show the scale or consequences of the issue. Narrow the discussion to the population and setting relevant to your study. Explain what is not yet known, inconsistent or adequately addressed, then state why resolving that gap matters."],
 ["A useful structure","A clear problem statement commonly moves from context, to evidence of the problem, to the specific knowledge gap, and finally to the consequence of leaving that gap unanswered. Avoid unsupported claims, exaggerated language and a long list of unrelated statistics."],
 ["Check alignment","Your problem statement should lead naturally to your research question, general objective and specific objectives. If the objectives do not address the gap you identified, revise the scope before writing further."],
 ["Common mistakes","Do not confuse a topic with a problem; claim that no research exists without searching; use statistics without a source; or promise that a small student project will solve a large health-system problem. Discuss feasibility and scope with your supervisor."]
 ]},
 {slug:"research-objectives-questions-hypotheses",title:"Research Objectives, Questions and Hypotheses",desc:"Understand how research questions, general objectives, specific objectives and hypotheses align.",sections:[
 ["Research question","The research question states what the study seeks to find out. It should identify the key concept or variables, population and context where appropriate. A focused question guides the design, eligibility criteria and analysis."],
 ["General objective","The general objective expresses the overall purpose of the study, usually beginning with an action verb such as determine, estimate, assess, explore or describe. It should answer the research question without promising more than the design can establish."],
 ["Specific objectives","Specific objectives divide the overall purpose into measurable or answerable components. Each should address one coherent task and should be reflected in the data collection tool and analysis plan."],
 ["Hypotheses","A hypothesis is a testable prediction about a relationship or difference. It is commonly used in analytical quantitative studies. Descriptive studies and many qualitative studies may not require a formal hypothesis."],
 ["Alignment check","Create a simple mapping: each specific objective should have corresponding variables or qualitative domains, a data source, and a planned analysis. Avoid adding objectives that the methods cannot answer."]
 ]},
 {slug:"quantitative-qualitative-mixed-methods",title:"Quantitative, Qualitative and Mixed-Methods Research",desc:"Compare research approaches and choose an approach that fits the question and type of evidence needed.",sections:[
 ["Quantitative research","Quantitative approaches use numerical measurements to describe distributions, estimate frequency or magnitude, and examine associations or effects. They require clearly defined variables, appropriate measurement and a planned statistical analysis."],
 ["Qualitative research","Qualitative approaches investigate meaning, experience, perceptions, processes and context. Data may come from interviews, focus groups or documents. Sampling is usually purposive or otherwise information-oriented, and analysis involves systematic coding and interpretation."],
 ["Mixed methods","Mixed-methods research intentionally combines quantitative and qualitative components. The researcher should explain why both are needed and how findings will be integrated, rather than simply collecting two types of data independently."],
 ["Choose from the question","Ask whether the question seeks to measure how much or how often, understand how or why people experience something, or combine both. Practical constraints matter, but convenience alone should not determine the design."],
 ["Quality considerations","Quantitative quality includes measurement validity, reliability, bias control and appropriate analysis. Qualitative quality includes transparent sampling, reflexivity, depth, credibility and a clear analytic trail."]
 ]},
 {slug:"cross-sectional-cohort-case-control-studies",title:"Cross-Sectional, Cohort and Case-Control Studies",desc:"Learn the logic, strengths and limitations of three common observational study designs.",sections:[
 ["Cross-sectional studies","A cross-sectional study measures exposure and outcome at a defined point or period. It is useful for estimating prevalence and describing patterns. Because exposure and outcome are often measured together, temporal sequence and causal inference are limited."],
 ["Cohort studies","A cohort study follows people classified by exposure status to observe subsequent outcomes. It can estimate incidence and clarify temporal sequence. Prospective cohorts collect data forward; retrospective cohorts use existing records, which must be accessed with appropriate permission and governance."],
 ["Case-control studies","A case-control study selects participants based on outcome status, then compares previous exposures between cases and controls. It is useful for uncommon outcomes or long latency periods. Selection of appropriate controls and accurate exposure measurement are important."],
 ["Choosing a design","Consider the research question, outcome frequency, time, cost, available population, ethical constraints and data quality. No design is automatically best; each answers a different type of question."],
 ["Interpretation","Observational associations do not by themselves prove causation. Consider confounding, selection bias, information bias, temporality and the limits of the sampling frame when interpreting findings."]
 ]},
 {slug:"sampling-methods-and-sample-size",title:"Sampling Methods and Sample-Size Planning",desc:"Understand target populations, sampling frames, probability and non-probability sampling, and sample-size assumptions.",sections:[
 ["Population and sampling frame","Define the target population to which you hope findings may apply. The accessible population is the group you can realistically reach. A sampling frame is the operational list or source from which participants are selected."],
 ["Probability sampling","Simple random, systematic, stratified and cluster sampling use known selection procedures. When properly implemented, they support statistical inference and estimation of sampling error."],
 ["Non-probability sampling","Convenience, consecutive, purposive and snowball approaches may be practical or appropriate for particular objectives. They do not give every eligible person a known selection probability, so generalisability must be considered carefully."],
 ["Sample-size assumptions","Sample size depends on the primary objective and design. A prevalence estimate may require an expected proportion, confidence level and precision; comparisons may require effect size, variability, power and allocation assumptions. Add a justified allowance for non-response where appropriate."],
 ["Report transparently","State the formula or software and assumptions, source of parameter estimates, sampling procedure, eligibility criteria and anticipated non-response. A sample-size number without its assumptions is not reproducible."]
 ]},
 {slug:"research-ethics-informed-consent",title:"Research Ethics and Informed Consent",desc:"A student guide to ethics review, voluntary participation, informed consent, confidentiality and participant protection.",sections:[
 ["Ethical review before research","Research involving people, identifiable information or biological material may require review by an authorised ethics committee and other permissions. Determine the applicable pathway with your institution before recruitment, accessing records or collecting data."],
 ["Informed consent","Consent is a process, not merely a signature. Participants should receive understandable information about purpose, procedures, risks, benefits, confidentiality, voluntariness, withdrawal and contacts for questions."],
 ["Privacy and confidentiality","Collect only information necessary for the approved objectives. Separate identifiers from study data where feasible, restrict access, use secure storage and follow the approved retention and destruction plan."],
 ["Vulnerable participants","Additional safeguards may be required where participants have limited autonomy or are at increased risk of coercion or harm. Recruitment should avoid undue influence and respect local context."],
 ["Regulatory permissions","Ethics approval, institutional permission and any required national research licence are distinct requirements. One does not automatically replace another. Confirm current requirements with the relevant authorities."]
 ]},
 {slug:"questionnaire-design-validity-reliability",title:"Questionnaire Design, Validity and Reliability",desc:"Develop clear research questions and understand content validity, reliability, piloting and instrument adaptation.",sections:[
 ["Start from objectives","Every questionnaire item should serve an objective or a defined variable. Build a table linking objectives, constructs, indicators and questions before drafting the instrument."],
 ["Write clear items","Use simple language, one idea per question and response options that are mutually understandable. Avoid leading, double-barrelled or unnecessarily sensitive questions. Specify recall periods where relevant."],
 ["Validity","Validity concerns whether an instrument measures what it is intended to measure. Content review, expert assessment and appropriate construct or criterion evidence may contribute, depending on the instrument and context."],
 ["Reliability","Reliability concerns consistency of measurement. Internal consistency statistics, test-retest assessment or inter-rater agreement may be relevant depending on the scale and measurement process. A high coefficient does not prove validity."],
 ["Pilot testing","Pilot the tool in a setting and population similar to the intended study, without treating pilot participants as part of the main sample unless the approved protocol allows it. Check comprehension, flow, timing, missing responses and data-entry feasibility."]
 ]},
 {slug:"descriptive-statistics-and-data-analysis",title:"Descriptive Statistics and Choosing an Analysis",desc:"A beginner-friendly guide to data types, descriptive summaries, confidence intervals and matching analysis to objectives.",sections:[
 ["Know your variables","Categorical variables describe groups or categories; numerical variables represent counts or measurements. Identify whether numerical data are discrete or continuous and consider the measurement scale before choosing summaries."],
 ["Describe categorical data","Use counts and proportions, with denominators stated clearly. For example, report the number and percentage of participants in each category and explain missing observations."],
 ["Describe numerical data","Mean and standard deviation are commonly used for approximately symmetric distributions. Median and interquartile range are often more informative for skewed data or ordinal measurements. Inspect distributions rather than choosing summaries mechanically."],
 ["Inference and uncertainty","Confidence intervals communicate the precision of estimates. P-values should be interpreted alongside effect sizes, confidence intervals, study design and clinical or practical importance; they do not measure the probability that a hypothesis is true."],
 ["Plan before testing","Select analysis based on the objective, variable types, design, distribution, independence and assumptions. Do not run many tests simply to find a statistically significant result. Document exclusions, missing-data handling and software."]
 ]},
 {slug:"writing-results-and-discussion",title:"How to Write Research Results and Discussion",desc:"Present findings accurately, distinguish results from interpretation and discuss limitations without overstating conclusions.",sections:[
 ["Results section","Present findings in the order of the objectives. Describe participant flow, key characteristics, primary outcomes and relevant secondary analyses. Tables should be understandable without forcing readers to search the text for definitions."],
 ["Use tables effectively","Give each table a clear title, define abbreviations, state units and denominators, and avoid duplicating every table value in prose. Report missing data where it affects interpretation."],
 ["Discussion section","Begin by summarising the principal findings in relation to the objectives. Compare them with relevant literature, explore plausible explanations and discuss similarities or differences without selectively citing only supportive studies."],
 ["Limitations","Explain important sources of bias, imprecision, measurement limitations, missing data and limits to generalisability. State how each could affect interpretation rather than listing generic limitations."],
 ["Conclusion and recommendations","Conclusions must follow from the results and design. Avoid causal claims from designs that cannot establish causation. Recommendations should be proportionate to the evidence and identify areas where further research is needed."]
 ]},
 {slug:"sample-size-calculation-prevalence-studies",title:"Sample-Size Calculation for Prevalence Studies",desc:"Learn the inputs, assumptions and interpretation behind sample-size calculations for estimating prevalence.",sections:[["Define the primary estimate","For a prevalence study, the main aim is usually to estimate the proportion of a defined population with a characteristic or condition during a specified period. Define the outcome, population and time frame before selecting a formula."],["Core single-proportion formula","A commonly taught starting point is n = Z²p(1−p) / d², where Z is the standard-normal value for the chosen confidence level, p is the anticipated prevalence and d is the desired absolute precision. The formula assumes a simple random sample and other stated conditions."],["Choose the prevalence estimate","Use a credible prior study or relevant surveillance estimate from a population and setting reasonably similar to yours. If no defensible estimate is available, p = 0.5 is sometimes used because it produces the largest sample under this formula. Explain the source or rationale."],["Precision and confidence","A smaller margin of error requires a larger sample. A higher confidence level also increases the required sample. Precision should be realistic in relation to available time, resources and the intended use of the estimate."],["Adjust for design and non-response","Cluster sampling may require a design-effect adjustment. Add an allowance for expected non-response or incomplete records using a clearly stated method. Finite population correction may be relevant when sampling a substantial fraction of a small, known population."],["Report assumptions transparently","State the formula, Z value, prevalence estimate, precision, design effect if used, non-response allowance and final target. Confirm the approach with your supervisor or statistician; other objectives may require a different sample-size calculation."]]},
 {slug:"pico-framework-clinical-research-questions",title:"Using the PICO Framework for Clinical Research Questions",desc:"Structure focused clinical questions using Population, Intervention, Comparison and Outcome.",sections:[["What PICO means","PICO is a framework commonly used to structure focused clinical questions: Population or Patient, Intervention or exposure, Comparison and Outcome. Not every question needs all four elements, but the framework helps make the question specific."],["Population","Describe who the question concerns, including relevant clinical characteristics, setting or age group. Avoid defining the population so narrowly that the study becomes infeasible without a clear reason."],["Intervention and comparison","For intervention questions, specify the treatment, procedure or programme and the comparator, such as usual care, placebo or another intervention. For observational questions, the exposure and reference group may take these roles."],["Outcome","Choose an outcome that is clinically meaningful and measurable. Specify how and when it will be assessed. Avoid listing many unrelated outcomes without identifying a primary outcome."],["Example and refinement","A draft question might ask whether a defined intervention, compared with usual care, reduces a specified outcome among a defined patient group over a stated period. Refine the wording to match the study design and available data."],["Limits of PICO","PICO is useful for many quantitative clinical questions, especially intervention and evidence-search questions. Qualitative questions about experiences may be better structured using frameworks such as PICo or SPIDER. Select a framework that fits the question."]]},
 {slug:"literature-review-search-strategy",title:"How to Conduct a Literature Review and Build a Search Strategy",desc:"Plan a reproducible search, screen relevant literature and synthesise evidence without simply listing papers.",sections:[["Define the review question","Clarify the topic, population, concepts and purpose of the review. A focused question helps determine which databases, keywords, inclusion criteria and types of evidence are relevant."],["Identify search concepts","Break the question into core concepts and list synonyms, spelling variants, abbreviations and controlled vocabulary where available. Combine synonyms within a concept using OR and combine distinct concepts using AND."],["Choose sources and document searches","Use appropriate scholarly databases and institutional library resources. Record the database, platform, search date, exact search string and filters so another reader can understand how the literature was identified."],["Screen consistently","Apply inclusion and exclusion criteria consistently. Remove duplicates, screen titles and abstracts, then assess full texts where required. Keep a record of reasons for full-text exclusions when conducting a structured review."],["Assess relevance and quality","Consider study design, setting, sample, measurement, risk of bias and relevance to your question. Do not treat every published paper as equally reliable or assume that publication alone establishes validity."],["Synthesise rather than list","Group studies by themes, methods, populations or findings. Compare agreements and contradictions, explain possible reasons for differences and identify what remains uncertain. Cite original sources accurately and avoid plagiarism."]]},
 {slug:"confounding-and-bias-clinical-research",title:"Confounding and Bias in Clinical Research",desc:"Understand common systematic errors, confounding variables and practical approaches to reducing bias.",sections:[["What bias means","Bias is a systematic error that can distort an estimate or interpretation. It differs from random error, which produces variation without a consistent direction. Bias can arise during participant selection, measurement, follow-up, analysis or reporting."],["Selection bias","Selection bias occurs when the way participants enter or remain in a study creates groups that differ in ways relevant to the research question. Define eligibility criteria clearly, use an appropriate sampling approach and describe participant flow."],["Information bias","Information bias arises from inaccurate or unequal measurement of exposure, outcome or other variables. Use clear operational definitions, validated tools where suitable, standardised procedures and training for data collectors."],["Confounding","A confounder is associated with the exposure and outcome, but is not an intermediate step on the causal pathway being studied. It can create, exaggerate, reduce or reverse an observed association."],["Control and analysis","Possible approaches include restriction, matching, stratification and multivariable adjustment. These methods require sound subject-matter reasoning and appropriate data. Statistical adjustment cannot automatically correct poorly measured or unmeasured confounding."],["Interpret findings cautiously","Describe important potential biases and how they may influence results. Observational findings should be interpreted in light of design, temporality, measurement quality and residual confounding; association alone does not prove causation."]]},
 {slug:"odds-ratios-risk-ratios-confidence-intervals",title:"Odds Ratios, Risk Ratios and Confidence Intervals",desc:"Interpret common measures of association and uncertainty in epidemiological research.",sections:[["Risk and odds","Risk is the proportion of people who experience an outcome over a defined period. Odds compare the probability that an event occurs with the probability that it does not. They are related but are not interchangeable."],["Risk ratio","The risk ratio compares risk in an exposed group with risk in an unexposed or comparison group. A value of 1 indicates equal observed risks; values above or below 1 indicate higher or lower risk in the exposed group, respectively."],["Odds ratio","The odds ratio compares the odds of an outcome between groups. It is commonly reported in case-control studies and logistic regression. When outcomes are common, an odds ratio can differ substantially from the risk ratio and should not be described as if it were a risk ratio."],["Confidence interval","A confidence interval expresses the precision of an estimate under the statistical model and sampling procedure. For ratio measures, an interval containing 1 is commonly described as compatible with no association at the corresponding two-sided significance level, subject to assumptions."],["Interpret magnitude and context","Consider the point estimate, interval width, direction, clinical or public-health importance, study design and possible bias. Statistical significance does not automatically imply an important effect or a causal relationship."],["Report responsibly","Name the measure, identify the comparison groups, give the estimate and confidence interval, and state the analysis context. Avoid interpreting an association without considering confounding and design limitations."]]},
 {slug:"chi-square-t-tests-logistic-regression",title:"Choosing Between Chi-Square Tests, t-Tests and Logistic Regression",desc:"Understand when common statistical methods may be appropriate and the assumptions that need checking.",sections:[["Start with the research objective","Choose a statistical method based on the question, study design, outcome and predictor types, distribution, independence and assumptions. Do not select a test simply because it is familiar or produces a small p-value."],["Chi-square test","The Pearson chi-square test assesses whether categorical variables are associated in a contingency table when expected cell counts are adequate. For sparse tables, an exact method or an appropriate alternative may be needed."],["Independent and paired t-tests","An independent-samples t-test compares means between two independent groups. A paired t-test compares paired measurements, such as before-and-after values in the same participants. Check distributional assumptions and the design; alternatives may be appropriate."],["Logistic regression","Binary logistic regression models the relationship between predictors and a binary outcome. It estimates coefficients commonly expressed as odds ratios. Consider sample size, model specification, multicollinearity, influential observations and the number of outcome events."],["Assumptions and diagnostics","Check independence, measurement quality, expected cell counts, distribution of residuals where relevant, variance assumptions and model fit. The required checks differ by method; software output does not replace statistical reasoning."],["Present results clearly","Report the method, variables compared, estimate, confidence interval and p-value where appropriate. Explain why the method fits the objective and acknowledge limitations. Seek statistical supervision for complex or high-stakes analyses."]]},
 {slug:"data-cleaning-spss",title:"Data Cleaning in SPSS: A Practical Student Workflow",desc:"A structured approach to checking, coding, documenting and preparing a research dataset in SPSS.",sections:[["Preserve the original dataset","Keep an unchanged copy of the raw data and work on a separate analysis copy. Use clear file names, dates and version control. Never overwrite the only original dataset."],["Create a data dictionary","Document each variable name, label, type, units, permitted values, coding and missing-value convention. Use consistent names and avoid ambiguous abbreviations."],["Check structure and coding","Inspect variable types, categories, ranges, duplicate identifiers and unexpected values. Confirm that categorical codes match the questionnaire or data-collection form. Do not assume a numeric code is a continuous measurement."],["Review missing data","Summarise missingness by variable and, where relevant, by participant group. Distinguish not applicable, not recorded, refused and unknown if the protocol defines them separately. Do not replace missing values arbitrarily."],["Check outliers and consistency","Use frequencies, descriptive summaries, plots and logical cross-checks to identify unusual values. Verify against source records only when authorised. Correct errors transparently; do not delete valid extreme observations merely because they affect results."],["Keep an audit trail","Document recoding, exclusions, derived variables and transformations. Save syntax where possible so procedures can be repeated. Follow the approved protocol and institutional data-management requirements."]]},
 {slug:"vancouver-referencing-medical-research",title:"Vancouver Referencing for Medical and Health Research",desc:"Learn the principles of numbered Vancouver-style citations and consistent reference-list preparation.",sections:[["How Vancouver style works","Vancouver is a numbered citation style widely used in biomedical writing. Sources are numbered in the order they first appear in the text, and the same source retains its original number when cited again."],["In-text citations","Place the citation number at the location required by your institution or target journal. Numbering and punctuation conventions can vary slightly between local guides, so follow the exact style requested."],["Journal article references","A typical journal reference includes author names, article title, journal title abbreviation, year, volume, issue where required and page range or article identifier. Verify bibliographic details against the original publication."],["Books and websites","Books generally require author or editor, title, edition where relevant, place and publisher, and year. Online sources may require a URL and access date depending on the style guide. Use reliable sources and record access details."],["Use reference-management tools carefully","Reference managers can reduce manual errors, but imported metadata may be incomplete or incorrect. Check author order, journal abbreviations, page numbers, DOI and punctuation against the source."],["Academic integrity","Cite ideas, data, figures and wording that are not your own. A reference list does not replace in-text attribution. Do not cite sources you have not checked or rely on fabricated references."]]},
 {slug:"writing-research-abstract",title:"How to Write a Structured Research Abstract",desc:"Prepare a concise abstract that accurately summarises the background, objective, methods, results and conclusion.",sections:[["Purpose and word limit","An abstract allows readers to understand the study quickly. Check the university, conference or journal word limit and required headings before drafting. Structured formats commonly use background, objective, methods, results and conclusion."],["Background and objective","Give only enough context to establish the problem, then state the specific objective. Avoid broad introductory claims that consume words without clarifying the study."],["Methods","Summarise the design, setting, population, sampling, key measurements and analysis. Include essential dates or follow-up duration where relevant, while keeping within the word limit."],["Results","Report the actual findings, including the relevant denominator and numerical estimates. Include effect estimates or confidence intervals when appropriate. Never invent results or write anticipated findings as if data have already been collected."],["Conclusion","State what the findings support, in proportion to the design and results. Avoid causal claims unsupported by the study and do not introduce new information that is absent from the results."],["Final checks","Ensure every number matches the main report, abbreviations are defined where required and the abstract can stand alone. Many institutions require the abstract to be revised after the full report is complete."]]},
 {slug:"research-defence-presentation-preparation",title:"Preparing for a Research Defence Presentation",desc:"Organise a clear presentation and prepare to explain your research decisions, findings and limitations.",sections:[["Know the assessment brief","Confirm presentation duration, slide limit, required sections, marking criteria and whether the defence includes questions from a panel. Follow the institution's instructions rather than a generic slide template."],["Build a logical slide sequence","A concise sequence often includes title, background and problem, objectives, methods, key results, discussion, limitations, conclusion and recommendations. Adapt it to the study and the assessment requirements."],["Show rather than overcrowd","Use readable fonts, meaningful headings and uncluttered tables or figures. Highlight the main message of each slide. Avoid copying paragraphs from the report onto slides."],["Explain methodological choices","Be ready to justify the design, setting, eligibility criteria, sampling, sample size, measurements, analysis and ethical safeguards. Understand the limitations and alternatives considered."],["Present results honestly","Use the actual findings and denominators. Explain uncertainty and unexpected results without hiding inconvenient findings or overstating statistical significance."],["Practise questions and timing","Rehearse aloud, time the presentation and practise concise answers. If you do not know an answer, explain what you know and how you would verify the point. The student must understand and defend their own work."]]},
];

